WEBVTT

00:00.263 --> 00:02.688
The page's database records say delivered_at

00:02.688 --> 00:06.088
declares TEXT and allows NULL, a missing value.

00:06.088 --> 00:08.997
In this SQLite table, the type declaration

00:08.997 --> 00:11.822
does not strictly enforce every stored value.

00:11.822 --> 00:14.111
What does a missing delivery time mean?

00:14.111 --> 00:15.807
Predict an answer before continuing.

00:20.750 --> 00:22.841
The column record cannot settle that.

00:22.841 --> 00:25.800
The parcel might be unfinished, the record might be

00:25.800 --> 00:29.572
awaiting an update, or the arrival time might be unknown.

00:29.572 --> 00:34.192
For this page's complete invented records, NULL means not yet delivered.

00:34.192 --> 00:37.474
Structure permits the value; the definition explains its meaning.

00:38.333 --> 00:40.679
Meaning can change without a schema change.

00:40.679 --> 00:42.890
A parcel arrives at twelve twenty.

00:42.890 --> 00:45.662
Against the original noon promise, it is late.

00:45.662 --> 00:49.314
Against a revised twelve thirty promise, it is on time.

00:49.314 --> 00:52.096
The column name and type can stay the same.

00:52.096 --> 00:53.988
We must know which promise is stored.

00:55.045 --> 00:57.214
Now inspect the saved query.

00:57.214 --> 01:01.950
A view is a named query; this SQLite view recalculates when read.

01:01.950 --> 01:05.929
Focus on its filter: keep rows whose delivery time is not missing.

01:05.929 --> 01:07.618
Which parcel will that remove?

01:07.618 --> 01:08.825
Pause and point to it.

01:13.583 --> 01:17.560
The unfinished parcel has the missing time, so the filter removes it.

01:17.560 --> 01:21.212
Three completed deliveries remain, including the late one.

01:21.212 --> 01:22.710
Two were on time.

01:22.710 --> 01:25.779
Two out of three gives 66.7%.

01:25.779 --> 01:30.308
The example uses complete records and the page's fixed UTC time format.

01:31.399 --> 01:36.052
Delivery Operations needs all four deliveries due, giving 50.0%.

01:36.052 --> 01:39.310
This view answers the completed-delivery question instead.

01:39.310 --> 01:43.178
The saved query describes the calculation: that is metadata.

01:43.178 --> 01:45.792
Its returned percentage is output data.

01:45.792 --> 01:49.775
Reading the query reveals what it counts; it does not choose the business rule.

01:50.878 --> 01:53.199
Try the page's metadata-map exercise.

01:53.199 --> 01:55.963
In Column's Example record, find delivered_at,

01:55.963 --> 01:58.562
its declared type, and its not-null value.

01:58.562 --> 02:02.971
Then select Saved query and compare its filter with Metric definition.

02:02.971 --> 02:07.781
Explain what the database permits, what the values mean, and what the query counts.

02:07.781 --> 02:11.912
The page keeps the full SQL and catalogue comparison for reference.
